cillin which is one of these  structurally related antibiotics it has  that beta-lactam ring and clavulanic  acid and so the two are there together  so let's look at how they work here I've  drawn the structure of penicillin and  related drugs so they've got slightly  different R groups here but otherwise  quite similar and now here is the  structure of cloudy ionic acid and in  red I've noted the similarities and so  you can see how much cloudy ionic acid  looks like penicillin and related drugs  and so the mechanism through which it  interferes with these beta lactam aces  is it actually binds to the active site  of beta lactam aces so what is the  active site to have an enzyme the active  site of an enzyme is the place where the  substrate binds for the enzyme to act on  so normally beta lactam aces would bind  penicillin and related drugs and cleave  that beta-lactam ring but instead the  beta lactamase will bind clavulanic acid  which is similar enough to its normal  substrates to find that active site but  dissimilar enough that the beta lactam  aces can't actually cleave the ring and  so it's a way of just kind of jamming  off the beta lactam aces you've got this  Club you land  I said sitting in the active site of the  beta lactamase so it can no longer in  activate the co-administered drug  whether that's penicillin or amoxicillin  or ampicillin or whatever and so  basically these blocks that enzyme from  being able to work so does that solve  all of our problems with antibiotic  resistance does that mean that we can  just administer penicillin and related  drugs easily now not exactly and the  reason for that is because the bacteria  are able to in some cases overcome this  CLAB Yelena acid defense for example  some bacteria exhibit increased  production of beta lactam Isis what that  means is they make so many beta lactam  aces but all of the clavulanic acid all  of the primal Anik acid in a drug can  get used up blocking active sites of all  of those bet